  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nLarge dome of high pressure over the southern CONUS/Gulf remains\nunwavering, spanning most of the southern US. The upper-level\nridge pattern overhead will keep conditions drier, but chances\nfor afternoon showers and thunderstorms will exist today through\nSunday with disturbances moving through the ridge creating a \ntypical 'dirty ridge' pattern. Convection will be limited to \ndiurnal heating or upslope forcing. Currently, severe weather \nremains low chance, but a isolated strong thunderstorm or two \ncould form Saturday or Sunday afternoon across the mountains\nwith upslope flow.\n\nA warming trend starts today as many locations across the\nlowlands reach the lower and mid 80s. By Sunday, mid to upper \n80s will be common. Upper 80s to lower 90s are on the board for\nthe typical warm spots in the lowlands on Monday and Tuesday\nwith the warming trend increasing into early next week where it\nwill then plateau. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA fast-moving and vigorous disturbance is slated to move through\nthe ridge aloft Monday afternoon. Scattered showers and\nthunderstorms will be possible in the late afternoon and \nevening. Currently, most of the severe weather environment and\ntiming remains to the west, but our OH, KY, and western WV\ncounties may see some stronger thunderstorms with CAPE values\napproaching 2,000 J/Kg. PWATs are being modeled between 1.75\"\nand 2.25\" which would lead to efficient rainfall that could\ncause flash flooding. Models are suggesting the heaviest rain \nwill occur across either the southern coalfields or the Tri-\nState Area/KY.",
